# Environmental Chemistry: Pollution, Climate, and Green Chemistry

> The environment is chemistry running at planetary scale: the air we
> breathe, the water we drink, the soil that feeds us. This lesson maps
> the major pollutants and their sources, the science of the greenhouse
> effect and acid rain, and the green-chemistry playbook for preventing
> pollution at the design stage instead of cleaning up afterwards.

## 1. Segments of the Environment

@diagram tree id="environment-segments" layout="tree" aria="Four segments of the environment"
  "Environment" -> "Atmosphere (air)"
  "Environment" -> "Hydrosphere (water)"
  "Environment" -> "Lithosphere (soil and rock)"
  "Environment" -> "Biosphere (living things)"
@/diagram

[definition]
A **pollutant** is any substance or form of energy whose concentration
in the environment is unfavourable to life, or whose presence there is
simply out of place. Pollutants arrive from **point sources** (a
factory outfall pipe) or **non-point sources** (fertiliser runoff
washed from a whole catchment).
[/definition]

| Classification | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| Biodegradable | sewage, paper, crop residues |
| Non-biodegradable | plastics, heavy metals, DDT |
| Primary pollutants | emitted directly: CO, SO₂, NO, particulates |
| Secondary pollutants | formed in the air: ground-level ozone, PAN, acid rain |

## 2. Air Pollution: The Major Pollutants

| Pollutant | Main sources | Harmful effects |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Carbon monoxide (CO) | incomplete fuel burning, vehicles | binds haemoglobin about 200 times better than O₂ — oxygen starvation |
| Sulphur dioxide (SO₂) | coal power plants, smelters | throat and lung irritation, acid rain |
| Oxides of nitrogen (NOₓ) | hot combustion in engines | smog, acid rain, breathing trouble |
| Particulate matter (PM2.5, PM10) | dust, smoke, diesel exhaust | deep lung penetration, heart disease |
| Tropospheric ozone (O₃) | secondary: sunlight on NOₓ + hydrocarbons | photochemical smog, crop damage |
| Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) | old refrigerants, aerosol propellants | destroy stratospheric ozone |

[note]
PM2.5 means particles under 2.5 micrometres across — fine enough to
slip past the airways' defences deep into the lungs, which is why daily
AQI bulletins single it out.
[/note]

## 3. Water Pollution and the Oxygen Ledger

| Pollutant | Source | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sewage and organic waste | homes, food processing | microbes consume dissolved O₂ — fish suffocate |
| Fertiliser runoff (N, P) | farms | algal blooms, eutrophication |
| Heavy metals (Hg, Pb, Cd) | batteries, plating, e-waste | nerve damage; Minamata disease from mercury |
| Pesticides (DDT) | agriculture | biomagnification up the food chain |
| Pathogens | untreated sewage | cholera, typhoid, jaundice |
| Thermal discharge | power plants | warm water holds less dissolved oxygen |

[definition]
**BOD (Biochemical Oxygen Demand)** is the oxygen, in milligrams,
consumed by micro-organisms while decomposing the organic matter in one
litre of water over five days at 20 °C. **COD (Chemical Oxygen
Demand)** is the oxygen equivalent consumed by *all* oxidisable matter,
using strong chemical oxidants.
[/definition]

Clean water carries a BOD below about 5 ppm; heavily polluted water can
exceed 100 ppm. COD always exceeds BOD because chemicals, not only
microbes, do the consuming.

## 4. Eutrophication and Soil Pollution

Eutrophication is fertiliser-fed water turning green and then dead:

1. nutrient runoff enriches a lake with nitrogen and phosphorus;
2. algae bloom across the surface and block the light below;
3. the algae die; decomposers multiply and strip dissolved oxygen;
4. fish and other aerobic life suffocate — the lake ages prematurely.

Soil pollution adds its own signature: persistent pesticides (DDT
breaks down over decades), heavy metals from industry and e-waste, and
excess nitrogen. DDT concentrates at each step up the food chain —
**biomagnification** — which famously thinned the eggshells of falcons
and eagles.

## 5. The Greenhouse Effect and Global Warming

The **greenhouse effect** is natural and good: gases that pass
sunlight in but absorb outgoing infrared keep Earth about 33 °C
warmer than an airless world would be. The problem is the
*enhancement* — burning fossil fuels keeps thickening that blanket.

| Gas | Global warming potential (100-year) | Main sources |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CO₂ | 1 (the reference) | fossil fuels, deforestation, cement |
| CH₄ | about 28 | ruminants, paddy fields, landfills, gas leaks |
| N₂O | about 265 | nitrogen fertilisers, combustion |
| SF₆ and F-gases | up to about 23,500 | electrical switchgear, refrigerants |

@chart donut title="Approximate share of man-made greenhouse gas emissions (%), CO₂-equivalent"
labels:
CO2
Methane
N2O
F-gases

data:
76
16
6
2
@/chart

Consequences roll out from there: sea-level rise through thermal
expansion and ice melt, fiercer heatwaves and cloudbursts, coral
bleaching, and shifting crop belts. The international response runs
from the UNFCCC (1992) through the Kyoto Protocol (1997) to the Paris
Agreement (2015), which aims to hold warming well below 2 °C.

## 6. Ozone Depletion

Stratospheric ozone (roughly 15–35 km up) absorbs the harshest UV-B.
CFCs were the saboteur: UV knocks a chlorine atom loose, and one such
atom can destroy thousands of ozone molecules.

[example]
The catalytic cycle: $Cl + O_3 \rightarrow ClO + O_2$, then
$ClO + O \rightarrow Cl + O_2$ — the chlorine atom returns untouched,
ready to attack the next ozone molecule. The catalyst is the poison.
[/example]

The Antarctic **ozone hole**, announced in 1985, drove the **Montreal
Protocol (1987)** — the first treaty ratified by every UN member — and
with CFCs phased out the layer is now slowly healing.

[info]
Ground-level ozone is a pollutant; stratospheric ozone is a shield.
The same molecule, opposite reputations, different altitudes.
[/info]

## 7. Acid Rain

Natural rain is already faintly acidic (pH about 5.6, from dissolved
CO₂). Acid rain means pH below about 5.6, driven by SO₂ and NOₓ
converting to sulphuric and nitric acid:

@math label="eq:acid-rain"
2SO_2 + O_2 \rightarrow 2SO_3,\quad SO_3 + H_2O \rightarrow H_2SO_4
@/math

| Victim | Damage |
| --- | --- |
| Marble and limestone buildings | "marble cancer": CaCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → a flaking gypsum crust (Taj Mahal) |
| Lakes and streams | pH falls; fish eggs fail to hatch |
| Forests | nutrients leached away; freed aluminium harms roots |
| Soils | microbes and earthworms suffer |

[tip]
When a question mentions marble, statues, or the Taj Mahal, answer
acid rain; when it mentions refrigerants or the ozone hole, answer
CFCs. Mixing these two is a very common slip.
[/tip]

## 8. Green Chemistry and Waste Management

[definition]
**Green chemistry** designs chemical products and processes that
reduce or eliminate the use and generation of hazardous substances —
prevention at the design stage rather than treatment afterwards.
[/definition]

| Principle (condensed) | Practice |
| --- | --- |
| Prevention | no waste beats treating waste |
| Atom economy | route as many reactant atoms as possible into the product |
| Safer solvents | water, or no solvent at all, where possible |
| Renewable feedstocks | biomass instead of petrochemicals |
| Catalysis | catalysts rather than stoichiometric reagents |
| Design for degradation | products that break down harmlessly |

The waste-management hierarchy runs the same way — reduce, reuse,
recycle, then recover energy, and only finally dispose. India's Solid
Waste Management Rules (2016) push source segregation into wet, dry,
and domestic-hazardous fractions at the doorstep.

@diagram tree id="waste-hierarchy" layout="tree" aria="Waste management hierarchy from reduce to dispose"
  "Waste management" -> "Reduce"
  "Waste management" -> "Reuse"
  "Waste management" -> "Recycle"
  "Waste management" -> "Recover (energy)"
  "Waste management" -> "Dispose (landfill)"
@/diagram

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Primary vs secondary pollutants | emitted directly vs formed in the air |
| CO hazard | binds haemoglobin about 200 times more than O₂ |
| BOD | oxygen used by microbes in 5 days at 20 °C; clean water below 5 ppm |
| Eutrophication | nutrients → algal bloom → oxygen crash |
| Greenhouse gases | CO₂, CH₄ (about 28×), N₂O (about 265×), SF₆ (huge) |
| Ozone | stratospheric shield, ground-level pollutant; Montreal Protocol 1987 |
| Acid rain | pH below 5.6, from SO₂ and NOₓ |
| Green chemistry | prevent waste, atom economy, catalysts, renewables |
| Waste order | reduce → reuse → recycle → recover → dispose |
